WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:09.060
[Dr. John Neff:] My name is Dr. John Neff, Assistant Professor of Pediatrics at Johns Hopkins.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:12.110
It is my feeling that it is no longer necessary

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:16.280
to perform routine preschool or infant smallpox vaccinations.

00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:22.890
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I'm Samuel Katz, Professor and Chairman of the Department of Pediatrics at Duke University.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.250
I believe that our nation's freedom from smallpox

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.100
rests upon the widespread use of smallpox vaccine.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:36.040
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] I'm Dr. Paul Wehrle. It is my job today to try to resolve these two opposed points of view.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:51.280
[Music]

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.240
[Host:] Welcome to the exploration of concepts
and controversies in modern medicine,

00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:08.900
one of a series of programs dedicated to examining
the uncertain,

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:13.350
candidly recognizing that much of today's
teaching is necessarily based upon opinion,

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:19.260
and that the opinions of eminent physicians
in a given field vary widely.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.770
The National Medical Audiovisual Center believes
that openly airing such opposing views

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:28.670
is a basic responsibility of medical communications.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:34.380
Dr. Paul Wehrle, Hastings Professor of Pediatrics at the University Of Southern California

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:38.820
School of Medicine, will act as moderator for this presentation.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.400
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] For many years the physicians of this country and the workers in public health

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.930
have been emphasizing the need for vaccination for children,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:58.660
and particularly infants and preschool children,
to provide a basic immunity against smallpox.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:04.680
We also have been urging immunization at intervals
for particular types of health workers,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:12.450
for transportation workers, and at intervals
we have also urged reimmunization of all citizens

00:02:13.309 --> 00:02:17.469
of this country, whether they plan international travel or not.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:25.029
The immunization against smallpox is the earliest and one of the most effective prophylaxis

00:02:25.029 --> 00:02:28.860
agents against any of the communicable diseases.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:33.609
However, during recent years the ballgame
seems to be changed,

00:02:33.609 --> 00:02:38.989
the rules are somewhat different, and perhaps
there are differences with respect to some

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:40.629
of the threats.

00:02:40.629 --> 00:02:46.359
I would first like to, before getting our
combatants into the fray,

00:02:46.359 --> 00:02:51.529
I'd like to first turn to see what's happening
with regard to the international picture

00:02:51.529 --> 00:02:56.609
and see what's happening to smallpox on the
worldwide scale because I think this is important

00:02:56.609 --> 00:03:00.629
to us as we develop our story today.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:09.180
As you'll see, in 1945 smallpox was present
throughout virtually all of South America.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:14.749
1945 you will note is the lighter, shaded
together with the darker shaded areas

00:03:14.749 --> 00:03:18.559
spread throughout all of South America,

00:03:18.559 --> 00:03:24.679
throughout all of Africa, through the Middle
East, through Southeast Asia,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.740
and down including Malaya

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:32.389
and many of the countries in the South Pacific.

00:03:32.389 --> 00:03:39.260
During the next period of time, from 1955
through 1965,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.729
we see additional changes taking place.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:49.560
Here the earlier period is in lighter color,
the subsequent period in darker color,

00:03:49.569 --> 00:03:54.929
and here you see that these areas originally
involved very solidly

00:03:54.929 --> 00:03:59.200
have become substantially smaller,

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:04.219
and I think it's important to note that the
areas involved here,

00:04:04.219 --> 00:04:06.329
although they are solidly colored,

00:04:06.329 --> 00:04:10.279
may have some differences with respect to
specific areas within those countries where

00:04:10.279 --> 00:04:12.749
smallpox remains a problem.

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:18.820
Also, another area that I hope we can point
up today is data concerning the risk of vaccination

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.300
should it be introduced into this country.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.840
I think at this time I would like to turn
to our first combatant this morning,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:39.280
Dr. Neff, and see how he would like to develop
his particular position.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.820
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, I think that there can
be no question now that smallpox vaccination

00:04:44.830 --> 00:04:45.819
has been the one agent

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:50.400
which has been responsible for eradicating
smallpox from large areas of the world.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.939
But I think there can be no question also
that by present-day standards,

00:04:54.939 --> 00:05:00.009
smallpox vaccine, or as a vaccine, is a very
poor vaccine.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:03.479
And I think for two reasons it's a poor vaccine.

00:05:03.479 --> 00:05:09.169
For one reason, the duration of immunity that is afforded by the smallpox vaccination is

00:05:09.169 --> 00:05:11.540
relatively short in duration

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.840
and probably absolute immunity
to smallpox from the vaccine is no longer

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:15.840
than three years.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:20.620
Number two, there are a significant number
of complications that are associated

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.620
with this particular vaccine,

00:05:22.629 --> 00:05:24.789
and a significant amount of mortality.

00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:31.430
Now, for a long time in this country we've
practiced routine preschool and infant vaccinations

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:35.550
so that within this country about 90 percent
of the people have been vaccinated at least

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:37.800
at least one time in their life.

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:43.840
And it's also true since 1949 there've been
no cases of smallpox imported into the United States.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.120
But for this we've paid a real price.

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:54.629
Based on surveys that we've done in 1968 and
1963, and review of the death certificates,

00:05:54.629 --> 00:05:58.589
there can be no question that there are at
least seven deaths a year

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.090
as a result of our smallpox vaccination policy.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.780
And the other question that this brings up,
has this really been the reason

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:08.139
that we have been free of smallpox?

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:11.379
If you look carefully in terms of the immunity
of the nation,

00:06:11.379 --> 00:06:16.680
you'll find that 80 percent of the population
has not been vaccinated within three years

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:19.430
and therefore are not absolutely protected
against smallpox,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:23.469
and 50 percent have not been vaccinated for
greater than 15 years

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:27.460
and probably have little or no protection
at all from smallpox.

00:06:27.460 --> 00:06:31.280
Whereas the epidemiology of smallpox is such that this can be prevented

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.680
from entering into our country,

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:38.090
and I think has been, not because our population
has been well-vaccinated,

00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:40.860
but because our travelers are well-vaccinated.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.759
And I think that the other thing is that it
is a very focal disease

00:06:44.759 --> 00:06:48.090
and that secondary cases can be predicted

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.230
and they're not going to sweep through the
nation as other diseases,

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:53.220
perhaps measles or whatnot.

00:06:53.229 --> 00:06:59.189
But it's the secondary cases are very focal
and occur in hospital personnel and family contact.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:03.669
And I think that all we have to do in this
country is number one, immunize our travelers

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:08.129
and that will keep smallpox out and number
two, identify those risks,

00:07:08.129 --> 00:07:12.620
those populations at high-risk and those are
your hospital personnel and your family contacts.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:18.039
And it is not necessary to perform routine
smallpox vaccination.

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:23.560
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Thank you Dr. Neff. I think you've summed up the arguments very nicely here.

00:07:23.569 --> 00:07:26.819
and I think at this point we should turn to
Dr. Sam Katz

00:07:26.819 --> 00:07:32.120
and see what he has to say concerning the
need to continue our present position.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:38.789
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I think that it's important
to recall that a little over 30 years ago,

00:07:38.789 --> 00:07:43.849
there were at least 15,000 cases of smallpox
in the United States.

00:07:43.849 --> 00:07:50.860
And that as Dr. Neff has pointed out, we have had
none basically over the last 20 years.

00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:58.229
This is an admirable record, and it's one
which I think we all wish to persist and defend.

00:07:58.229 --> 00:08:06.159
The question which is basic to this is weighing
a known dimension against the conjecture.

00:08:06.159 --> 00:08:12.659
We agree, I think, Dr. Wehrle, Dr. Neff and
I, that much of this admirable record stems

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:15.099
from the widespread use of vaccine.

00:08:15.099 --> 00:08:22.249
We cannot provide any data on what would happen if we abandoned this widespread immunization program.

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:25.900
We can look at the experience in other countries.

00:08:25.900 --> 00:08:32.039
In 1948 Great Britain abandoned the legal
compulsion of smallpox vaccination.

00:08:32.039 --> 00:08:37.579
Over subsequent years they've had hundreds of cases of smallpox, which have resulted from importation

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:44.500
and in this regard I would disagree with Dr.
Neff that the vaccination of our travelers

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:46.690
will protect us against importations,

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:52.690
because in most cases, it has not been natives
returning to their nation who have brought

00:08:52.690 --> 00:08:54.190
the initial case,

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:59.990
it has been a native of a country in which
the disease remains endemic,

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:03.160
as shown on Dr. Wehrle's chart.

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:06.110
In this regard then, I think we cannot close
the door

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:10.260
and rely solely upon the immunization of our
travelers.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:14.920
I think we have to accept the fact that we
can't control a great deal of the population

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:21.170
and that in the jet age it's very simple for
a person to be incubating smallpox,

00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:26.630
come from Asia, Africa, or Brazil to the United
States with no overt disease,

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:31.370
and introduce the disease within a very short
period of time.

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:35.870
The other feature is, I think our physicians
are unable to recognize the disease anymore

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:37.430
because we're free of it.

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:42.490
So this reliance on quick smothering of an
outbreak is, I think, a bit optimistic

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.720
under present circumstances.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:52.779
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] I think that Dr. Katz has
summed up very nicely many of the arguments

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:55.600
for continuing our present policies

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:02.000
and I think we have several points that we
ought to discuss in depth

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.639
in order to bring out some of these divergent
types of opinions.

00:10:06.639 --> 00:10:12.370
Now, the first question that I think we might
bounce around a bit concerns the probability

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:14.459
of introducing smallpox

00:10:14.459 --> 00:10:21.910
into the United States with our present surveillance
systems, with our present levels of immunization

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:23.180
and so on.

00:10:23.180 --> 00:10:25.899
And I think the Communicable Disease Center
in Atlanta,

00:10:25.899 --> 00:10:31.179
a number of other organizations such as World
Health Organization,

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:33.620
the governments and health services of many
other countries,

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:36.449
have studied this problem very carefully.

00:10:36.449 --> 00:10:41.899
John, you seem to minimize the risk of bringing
smallpox in,

00:10:41.899 --> 00:10:45.540
via either Americans abroad or nationals from
other countries.

00:10:47.100 --> 00:10:49.680
[Dr. John Neff:] Yes, well I think the important
point here,

00:10:49.690 --> 00:10:51.470
and I would disagree with Dr. Katz,

00:10:51.470 --> 00:10:55.680
it's not the nationals of another country,
but it's generally the nationals of the country

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:58.190
to which smallpox is introduced.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:03.040
I think one of the reasons is that smallpox
now in the present pattern of eradication

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:06.769
is being driven back into relatively remote
areas.

00:11:06.769 --> 00:11:10.049
And it is not the cities and it's not the
travelers that have a tendency

00:11:10.049 --> 00:11:13.540
to come in contact with cases of smallpox,

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:19.459
and I think that one can look at our own history
for the past 20 years.

00:11:19.459 --> 00:11:24.459
Our protection has not prevented smallpox
from spreading in the United States,

00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:26.130
actually we've never been challenged.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:28.519
We haven't had an importation into the United
States.

00:11:28.519 --> 00:11:30.740
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] How do you know we haven't
been challenged?

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:34.100
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, this is a good question.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:37.910
I think that we have a very good surveillance
system at the CDC,

00:11:37.910 --> 00:11:44.320
at the national CDC, and any suspected case
is looked at by a person

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.370
who is capable of diagnosing smallpox and
differentiating this from chickenpox.

00:11:48.370 --> 00:11:50.220
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I can't agree with that
now, John.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:54.920
I think you can be honest about this without
denigrating the medical profession.

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:59.410
We have not had the experience in this country
looking at smallpox.

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:05.800
Even the best physicians acquainted with tropical
medicine would be perfectly frank to say that

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:07.610
a bad case of chickenpox

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:14.240
is impossible to differentiate from a mild
or a more severe case of smallpox.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.839
I think we can all recall a few years ago
when this was exactly what happened,

00:12:18.839 --> 00:12:23.430
when a lady from Ghana was thought to have
smallpox and actually had Varicella.

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:26.959
Secondly, that emphasized the fact that our
laboratory facilities,

00:12:26.959 --> 00:12:32.389
though they may in rare instances be attuned
to this, are not constantly challenged with

00:12:32.389 --> 00:12:34.910
making the diagnosis of smallpox,

00:12:34.910 --> 00:12:39.050
so that there can be confusion even with the
laboratory support that's required

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:42.819
for the diagnosis, in a country where smallpox
is not occurring.

00:12:42.819 --> 00:12:48.919
So I have no peace of mind about our ability
to diagnosis smallpox

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:52.180
either clinically or laboratory-wise

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:59.190
at the drop of a hat in New York, San Francisco, Seattle, Boston, any port where the disease

00:12:59.190 --> 00:13:00.199
might enter tomorrow.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:06.380
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Well, this is certainly
an area where I think we might remain far apart.

00:13:06.540 --> 00:13:13.399
I wonder if there are a two or three things
that we could agree with here now?

00:13:13.399 --> 00:13:17.839
Certainly we haven't had any widespread occurrence of  smallpox

00:13:17.839 --> 00:13:19.899
in this country for many years.

00:13:19.899 --> 00:13:22.050
We're agreed on this point, we agree.

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:29.420
Now it is possible that a case or two cases
or an occasional case, one or two, three a year,

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:34.949
something like this, may come into the country, may occur without spread,

00:13:34.949 --> 00:13:36.910
could conceivably misdiagnosis.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:43.340
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I would say this again,
it's only conjecture, but to me, if I wanted

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:47.440
to use such conjecture I would use it to reinforce
the concept that

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:53.019
if this is happening periodically, our level
of immunization has been sufficient to dampen

00:13:53.019 --> 00:13:54.449
the spread of disease.

00:13:54.449 --> 00:13:57.209
I don't like John's use of the word a poor
vaccine.

00:13:57.209 --> 00:14:00.089
There are problems with smallpox vaccine.

00:14:00.089 --> 00:14:03.540
I think there is good work going on to improve
the vaccine.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:08.199
But this business of three years and prevention
of disease is one thing,

00:14:08.199 --> 00:14:10.110
prevention of death is the other.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:14.790
You're emphasizing death as your big fear
with smallpox vaccine.

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:18.639
I think you must emphasize death as your fear
with the disease.

00:14:18.639 --> 00:14:26.019
Now the mortality rate with importation in
Europe has been anywhere from 10 to 40 percent

00:14:26.019 --> 00:14:27.769
in various outbreaks.

00:14:27.769 --> 00:14:32.579
As far as protection from smallpox vaccine
is concerned,

00:14:32.579 --> 00:14:38.480
if memory serves me correctly, if you've had
immunization even within 20 years,

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:42.120
though you may get disease, there has not
been mortality from the disease.

00:14:42.120 --> 00:14:45.709
And I think in the long run that's our best
protection,

00:14:45.709 --> 00:14:47.079
is against death from the disease.

00:14:47.079 --> 00:14:50.019
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, let me mention something
here,

00:14:50.019 --> 00:14:56.670
I think that when you're talking about the
deaths secondary to smallpox vaccine,

00:14:56.670 --> 00:15:00.300
that you're talking in the range of seven a year,

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:04.040
which means over the 20 years that we
have been free of smallpox,

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:09.089
we've had probably in the range of 100 to
200 deaths as the result of the vaccine.

00:15:09.089 --> 00:15:14.350
It would take an awfully large epidemic of
smallpox to be able to equal that mortality.

00:15:14.350 --> 00:15:18.899
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Well John, before we get
into that area, let's clear up one or two

00:15:18.899 --> 00:15:21.550
points where I think we have some real problems.

00:15:21.550 --> 00:15:27.029
One of them is in the area of the real effectiveness
of this vaccine,

00:15:27.029 --> 00:15:32.540
and Dr. Katz indicated that the immunity as
far as death is concerned seems to be pretty solid.

00:15:33.079 --> 00:15:36.569
For at least 20 or 25 years after vaccination,

00:15:36.569 --> 00:15:38.309
death would be unusual.

00:15:38.360 --> 00:15:44.640
Cases do occur, but there is some protection
still conferred.

00:15:44.649 --> 00:15:49.920
After 10, 15, 20 or even 25 years you have
a diminished risk of acquiring the disease,

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.480
although for three years or something like
that it's virtually solid.

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:55.279
This really is a pretty good vaccine.

00:15:55.279 --> 00:16:01.930
I think what you meant was a poor vaccine
in terms of hazards in comparison with benefits

00:16:01.930 --> 00:16:04.870
in this particular frame, in this country.

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:08.300
[Dr. John Neff:] Right, and I think if you
are just going to look at that aspect alone,

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:13.050
then I think it would be very surprising if
this particular vaccine

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.600
passed the stringent regulations that we have
for a vaccine.

00:16:16.600 --> 00:16:18.879
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Well, let's look at the
deaths that occur.

00:16:18.879 --> 00:16:23.870
It seems to me that as we examine them, and
this is from Dr. Neff's own publications,

00:16:23.870 --> 00:16:24.560
[laughter]

00:16:24.560 --> 00:16:30.839
that at least 50 percent of them are in a
true sense preventable.

00:16:30.839 --> 00:16:34.659
That is, individuals receive smallpox vaccine

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:38.659
for whom we know it is an untoward immunization procedure,

00:16:40.379 --> 00:16:46.119
a child with eczema or the sibling of a child with eczema, a patient with a malignancy

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.040
involving the immune mechanism,

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:50.180
the patient on immunosuppressive drugs,

00:16:50.180 --> 00:16:54.800
these are patients we know will have trouble with smallpox vaccine,

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:58.170
these latter ones, or any live-virus vaccine.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:03.970
This is an accident of medical supervision
or of public health supervision.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:07.339
I don't think the vaccine should be incriminated
on this basis,

00:17:07.339 --> 00:17:12.960
rather we should incriminate ourselves as
having poor judgment in using it improperly.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:18.020
As far as the children are concerned with
eczema, I think we're aware

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:20.320
that there are efforts to improve

00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:25.330
and to come up with a strain of vaccinia that might not cause eczema vaccinatum.

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:30.710
But I would again get back to the point of
blanketing importations.

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:35.680
It seems to me that the sort of crash program
you have, when suddenly the news hits that

00:17:35.680 --> 00:17:38.270
a case of smallpox has been imported,

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:42.390
you're more apt to get poor discrimination in the use of vaccine

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:44.260
as you get the panic situation,

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:47.690
run around and vaccinate everyone who might
have been exposed...

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:51.570
it seems to me there you're in a situation where you're more likely

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:54.420
to immunize the inappropriate individuals

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:58.710
than you are in an ongoing program where good
judgment can be encouraged.

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:01.670
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, there are two things
that you bring up.

00:18:01.670 --> 00:18:04.600
One is the preventability of the complications.

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:08.240
And Sam, I have taken your stand in the past,

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:12.560
that a large number of these complications are preventable.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:18.910
But the thing that bothers me is that when you actually look at the complications that occur,

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:22.490
that could be preventable, this has been known
a long time,

00:18:22.490 --> 00:18:26.550
that you shouldn't vaccinate a child with
eczema or a family contact with eczema,

00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:32.300
yet at the same time between 1963 and 1968
when there's been a lot of publicity,

00:18:32.300 --> 00:18:35.760
you still have the same number of cases of
eczema vaccinata.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.400
The same can be said about vaccinating people

00:18:38.400 --> 00:18:41.780
with any type of immunological deficiency disease.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:47.410
But the difference between '63 and '68 in
the data when you would hopefully see a reduction,

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:51.340
there seems to be no change and it's just
an accident, I think,

00:18:51.340 --> 00:18:53.390
of practice that some of these people get
vaccinated.

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:57.690
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] Well then, you're willing to take that inevitability of change and bargain that

00:18:58.240 --> 00:19:02.850
against the unknown factor of all right, we're defeated, we can't alter that, is what you're

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:03.990
saying basically.

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:07.220
Are you then willing to bargain from the unknown,

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:11.750
which is the only way to prevent those few
deaths, which are tragic, I agree,

00:19:11.750 --> 00:19:18.030
but the only way to prevent it is then to
take the gamble, which we can't provide any data on

00:19:18.040 --> 00:19:26.720
of how many cases of smallpox would we have,
were we to do away with routine immunization

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.760
and how many cases of these same complications

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:33.080
would we have in the use of crash program vaccinations?

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:35.480
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] This brings up a very interesting
point

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:41.580
that I think we ought to try to cover in our
next session here.

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:45.210
I think one is why hasn't there been a decrease,

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:50.380
because everyone knows that you shouldn't
vaccinate a child with eczema,

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:55.150
and why are we continuing to have this same
frequency of involvement here?

00:19:55.150 --> 00:20:00.150
I think another thing that I'd like to see
you two spar over is,

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:04.140
is there really a greater risk in vaccinating
the individual

00:20:04.140 --> 00:20:11.740
who has a primary vaccination as an adolescent
or as a young adult?

00:20:11.740 --> 00:20:16.370
Because here is another part of the argument
and I think an important part.

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:21.530
If, for example, you're going have an unusually
high risk of complications

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:26.330
in vaccinating young adults for overseas travel,
for whatever,

00:20:26.330 --> 00:20:30.820
then whatever position we take I think has
to take that part into consideration.

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:37.630
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I think your implication,
I would accept it to support my argument,

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:39.520
[laughter]

00:20:39.520 --> 00:20:42.440
we do from the Netherlands, we do from other countries

00:20:42.440 --> 00:20:46.580
where those complications that are not preventable,

00:20:46.580 --> 00:20:50.740
encephalitis would be the one we would say, maybe we can prevent eczema vaccinatum.

00:20:50.740 --> 00:20:55.750
We don't know enough about post-vaccinal encephalitis
to even discuss its pathogenesis.

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:57.340
We don't know how to prevent it.

00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:02.580
We do have at least data which suggest that the older the age group having their primary experience,

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:08.420
the more likely one is to encounter post-vaccinal encephalitis.

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:14.300
I would certainly use that as an argument in favor of continuing childhood or infant immunizations.

00:21:14.540 --> 00:21:18.020
[Dr. John Neff:] Well Sam, I think that you've got to talk about the figures

00:21:18.020 --> 00:21:22.520
that come from the United States because there's a tremendous amount of discrepancy in terms of...

00:21:22.550 --> 00:21:24.530
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] We don't have the data
from the United States.

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:28.040
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, I think we do, Sam,
to some degree.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:31.280
It's true that right now about 90 percent
of your adults have been vaccinated,

00:21:31.280 --> 00:21:34.200
so you see very few primary.

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:38.150
But over the past 20 years since the Second
World War,

00:21:38.150 --> 00:21:42.680
about 10 percent of your military recruits
are primary vaccinees

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:47.430
and this comes to about two million military
recruits that have been vaccinated.

00:21:47.430 --> 00:21:52.030
And it happens to be that there have been
no reported cases of post-vaccinal encephalitis

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:54.120
since the Second World War in that group.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:57.120
Now it's possible that maybe one or two have
been missed,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.280
but you certainly don't have the same type
of experience

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:02.500
that they've been having in the Netherlands where one per thousand times...

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:06.540
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] What is this data now again on how many are truly primary vaccinees?

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:09.580
[Dr. John Neff:] About 10 percent, Sam, 10
to 13 percent.

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:12.580
So that if you work this out over a 20 year period,

00:22:12.580 --> 00:22:15.780
it comes in the range of 20 million primary vaccinations

00:22:15.790 --> 00:22:19.190
in a system where you have pretty good surveillance
for complications.

00:22:20.500 --> 00:22:22.680
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] I can't quite follow how
you're getting 20 million,

00:22:22.690 --> 00:22:27.000
[Dr. John Neff:] I'm sorry, two million. Excuse
me, Sam, two million.

00:22:27.000 --> 00:22:28.790
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] Yeah, yeah.

00:22:28.790 --> 00:22:33.740
If you turn around the other way, of course
your statistics on the severe complications

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:37.200
in childhood amount to about one per million.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:38.940
[Dr. John Neff:] That's right.

00:22:38.940 --> 00:22:43.430
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] And of those, one, we would
say 50 percent perhaps are preventable,

00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:50.410
so it's a half of one per million which brings
it down to probably the same rate

00:22:50.410 --> 00:22:51.700
I think you're bargaining for.

00:22:51.700 --> 00:22:53.480
[Dr. John Neff:] Well, it's variable.

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:55.990
I think that it's at least a half per million.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:23:00.430
In 1963 there were five post-vaccinal encephalitis
for six million primary,

00:23:00.430 --> 00:23:03.610
so this is merely one per million primary
vaccinations.

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:08.700
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] And this was for primary,
presumably in young age

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:09.770
[Dr. John Neff:] Right.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:11.120
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Individuals, symptoms.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:12.470
[Dr. John Neff:] Right, particularly.

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:19.020
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] Well, I would certainly
submit that we have difficulty in transposing

00:23:19.020 --> 00:23:21.120
data from one nation to another,

00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:23.980
but I'm tremendously impressed with the reliability

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:26.980
of the data from the Netherlands with their experience

00:23:26.990 --> 00:23:33.240
with armed forces groups who did not have
immunization against smallpox in childhood,

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:38.720
and their data is certainly very striking
as to the difference in incidence of post-vaccinal

00:23:38.720 --> 00:23:43.890
encephalitis when groups in their late teens
or early 20s are first immunized.

00:23:43.890 --> 00:23:49.160
I don't know that we have that sort of experience
in an ongoing prospective fashion.

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:53.000
They've directed attention to this, as we know Dr. Vandenorder [?]

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:55.100
and his associates have followed this carefully.

00:23:55.110 --> 00:23:58.460
I don't know that we've had any such careful surveillance.

00:23:58.460 --> 00:24:02.320
[Dr. John Neff:] No, but I think that say,
in Great Britain, there has been careful surveillance

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:08.110
and for, their experience is very much analogous
to what our experience has been.

00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:12.030
And theirs for revaccinees, uh, for primary
vaccinees in the adults

00:24:12.030 --> 00:24:16.940
it's in the range of 2.8 cases of post-vaccinal
encephalitis per million,

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:20.180
so this is a little bit more than we have
here,

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:22.970
but it's at least in our ballpark rather than
the Netherlands.

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:27.200
So it depends on what countries' data you
quote,

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:30.540
and I think that when we're making these decisions
you have to look at your own country

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:33.720
and make the decisions as best you can based
on our experience.

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:38.420
[Dr. Samuel Katz:] It puts us back in the
same place, if we look to our country,

00:24:38.420 --> 00:24:42.050
we can only look for conjectures or extrapolations.

00:24:42.050 --> 00:24:44.150
We don't have the comparative data.

00:24:44.150 --> 00:24:48.810
I suppose the question we're asking one another
is do we want to acquire it?

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:54.060
Are we at a point where we can justify a change
which will provide us with the data?

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:59.520
Are we too concerned about sticking pat the
way we are?

00:24:59.520 --> 00:25:03.460
Or are we going to get in trouble if we try
the other way?

00:25:06.260 --> 00:25:10.680
[Dr. Paul Wehrle:] Thank you, Dr. Katz, for summarizing very nicely the various

00:25:10.680 --> 00:25:13.240
points of view and information

00:25:13.240 --> 00:25:17.780
behind the present position regarding the
use of this vaccine.

00:25:17.780 --> 00:25:20.010
And thank you, Dr. Neff, for doing a beautiful
job

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:25.390
in summarizing the arguments toward changing
our position.

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:31.380
I think that we, in considering the present
posture with regard to the use of vaccinia

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:36.610
and the prevention of smallpox as a routine
procedure in this country,

00:25:36.610 --> 00:25:43.870
must keep these various points that have been
very ably presented very firmly in mind.

00:25:43.870 --> 00:25:50.330
One part of the argument revolves around the
likelihood of introduction of smallpox cases

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:51.430
into this country

00:25:51.430 --> 00:25:55.490
and the probability of spread once introduced.

00:25:55.490 --> 00:26:01.680
The data here I think are quite clear, in
that it seems unreasonable to assume that

00:26:01.680 --> 00:26:03.240
this is going to be something

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:05.570
that will be happening frequently in the future.

00:26:05.570 --> 00:26:10.110
However, the chance occurrence still remains
a possibility.

00:26:10.110 --> 00:26:15.620
The second area that was brought out, I think
very nicely by our combatants,

00:26:15.620 --> 00:26:22.170
is the problem of a risk associated with the
use of this most effective vaccine.

00:26:22.170 --> 00:26:25.660
A number of patients have developed,

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:31.360
and they can be expected to continue to develop,
serious complications.

00:26:31.360 --> 00:26:35.640
Another considerable risk that I think was
very important to bring out is what will happen

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:40.890
if we no longer immunize infants and are immunizing
only those

00:26:40.890 --> 00:26:44.140
adults who are involved in international travel

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:47.830
and other situations that might put them at
risk.

00:26:47.830 --> 00:26:52.840
Thank you very much for the opportunity to
be with you.

00:26:56.280 --> 00:26:59.340
[Host:] We thank Dr. Samuel Katz, Dr. John
Neff, and Dr. Paul Wehrle

00:26:59.350 --> 00:27:03.910
for their interesting analysis of a critical
problem in patient care.

00:27:03.910 --> 00:27:06.610
In subsequent programs we shall continue to
record

00:27:06.610 --> 00:27:10.620
equally significant concepts and controversies
in modern medicine.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:14.980
The opinions expressed on this program do
not necessarily constitute endorsement

00:27:14.980 --> 00:27:19.820
by the Department of Health, Education and Welfare, the Public Health Service, or its constituents.

00:27:20.000 --> 00:27:48.110
[Music]

